## The Billion-Dollar Mistake

Tony Hoare called null references his "billion-dollar mistake." XIOM uses contracts to make null safe:

```xiom
fn deref(ptr: *Int) -> Int
  requires: ptr != null;
{ return ptr; }  // Safe: contract guarantees non-null
```

### Non-Null by Default
In pure XIOM code, references (&T) can NEVER be null. Null only appears with raw pointers (*T):

```xiom
fn safe_func(data: &Vec[Int]) -> Int {
  // data can NEVER be null — &Vec[Int] excludes null
  return data.len();
}

fn unsafe_func(data: *Vec[Int]) -> Int
  requires: data != null;  // Must check — *Vec[Int] could be null
{ return data.len(); }
```

### Optional Pointers
```xiom
fn maybe_deref(ptr: *Int) -> Option[Int] {
  if ptr == null { return None(); }
  return Some(ptr);
}
```

### The Contract Pattern
Every function taking a *T parameter should have requires: param != null as its first line.

syntax_ref:
fn process(ptr: *T)
  requires: ptr != null;
{ let value = ptr; }

